def build(course, m):
    return course(95, 'Blue Finance and Community Benefit Assessment',
        'Blue Finance and\nCommunity Benefit\nAssessment',
        'Screen a fictional coastal proposal for distribution, affordability and the evidence behind its environmental and social claims.', [
        m('Define the project and its finance terms',
            'Create a basic financing and dependency map for a fictional coastal proposal.',
            """
A blue finance proposal needs a clear activity, a defined place and evidence of how the activity affects people and marine ecosystems. A descriptive label can indicate an intended sector. Ask what is funded, when resources are available, who controls decisions and which costs are excluded. Identify anyone expected to provide labour, space or services without a stated budget. [S84]

Distinguish funding for an asset from funding to operate it. A grant might buy a cold room while the users pay for electricity, maintenance and access. A loan could require repayment before seasonal sales recover. An investor may ask for control or a revenue share. For each source, write down the purpose restrictions, timing, responsibility and conditions in the supplied fictional terms. [S84]

Check what the proposal assumes about power supply, use, affordability and maintenance. Reducing post-harvest loss can benefit households and reduce pressure to replace spoiled stock. The outcome depends on effective use and conditions at the site. Compare the intended benefit with the practical ability to reach and afford the service. Ask whether small lots and informal workers can participate. [S81]

Use invented amounts and identify whether each represents a commitment or an assumption. Suppose the equipment cost is covered but operating costs of eight units a period are unassigned. That creates a recurring financing gap. Identify who can verify the terms and which small test could examine demand and access without committing real resources. State anomalies for the responsible team to resolve. [S84] List which responsibilities are agreed and which remain assumptions before asking anyone to judge the proposal.
            """,
            'A grant buys a cold room. The proposal calls it fully funded, although eight units of ongoing costs have no owner or confirmed revenue.',
            'Prepare a purpose, timing and obligation table. State the recurring gap and the feasibility questions to resolve.',
            'Separate acquisition and recurring costs. Name who would operate the facility and verify access, fees and continuity.',
            """
Identify the financed activity.|Which first question clarifies a coastal finance proposal?|What activity and costs will the money cover?|What colour is used on its logo?|How many headings are in the proposal?|Which award has the longest name?|Finance appraisal begins with scope and purpose.
Separate capital and operations.|What remains unfunded if equipment is covered and no operating source is assigned?|Electricity, maintenance and other recurring costs.|The equipment's recorded purchase cost.|The already paid capital invoice.|Only the meeting attendance list.|A purchase award does not necessarily meet operating costs.
Map obligations in time.|Why compare repayment dates with seasonal cash receipts?|A payment can fall due before earnings arrive.|Seasonal earnings guarantee repayment.|Initial capital equals available cash forever.|The timetable can replace all income evidence.|Cash timing affects feasibility.
Examine actual access.|Which test checks whether the cold room helps small processors?|Check small-lot fees, opening hours and capacity against their work pattern.|Count investors who joined the announcement.|Assume identical uptake among all workers.|Measure the ribbon length at launch.|Benefit depends on practical affordability and use.
Quantify the uncovered commitment.|What is the gap per period in the supplied capital-only case?|Eight units.|Zero units.|All historical sales.|A national budget estimate.|The recurring funds assigned are zero while recurring cost is eight.
            """),
        m('Screen environmental and distributional claims',
            'Distinguish an environmental claim from its evidence and specify who gains or pays.',
            """
Start with the claimed environmental gain and the path that might produce it. A cold room can reduce spoilage if it works reliably and people can use it. Its electricity source, backup arrangements and equipment maintenance may also affect environmental performance. A short observation needs careful interpretation. State the baseline, period, expected change and evidence required. [S84]

Ask which groups use the resource and who could lose access or incur costs. Fees, operating hours and admission terms can shape benefit as much as equipment capacity. A capture fisheries study uses different measures for landed value, household nutrition and work. These distinctions help a learner avoid equating gross receipts with wellbeing across coastal households. [R91]

Look beyond the project's own site. Nearby developments may constrain alternative landings or access routes. A proposal may report better conditions inside a managed zone while transferring effort or congestion outside it. Describe the possible mechanism and the site evidence needed. An indicator showing less activity within the project boundary can coexist with an increase elsewhere. [S82] [S84]

Write an appraisal note with headings for claim, evidence, uncertainty and decision. Include one affected group with limited influence and one possible adverse consequence. Identify who can verify habitat or safety claims and which evidence a funder could make a condition of consideration. In the classroom use only supplied evidence and compare the project with a practical alternative serving the same need. [S84] If the evidence uses a short period with favourable weather, report that timing and check whether a comparable period leads to a different result.
            """,
            'The operator uses lower spoilage to claim an overall environmental gain. Diesel use grew, some workers cannot pay and another landing is congested.',
            'Separate supported and unsupported claims. Identify evidence for net effects, distribution and nearby impacts.',
            'Lower spoilage matters. Check energy and displaced activity independently and explain which workers can afford access.',
            """
Specify a mechanism.|Which evidence relates directly to a spoilage-reduction claim?|Comparable spoiled quantities relative to handled stock over a stated period.|A branding announcement.|The number of investors at launch.|The size of the storage building alone.|Evidence needs a baseline, unit and consistent denominator.
Check additional environmental pressures.|Which concern can qualify the claimed improvement?|Fuel use and other environmental costs of operating the facility.|The logo has fewer colours.|The new equipment has a shorter name.|Total staff attendance at launch.|Operational environmental effects need evidence.
Distinguish receipts and benefits.|Why can't project sales alone establish household wellbeing?|Sales omit distribution, expenses and several livelihood dimensions.|Sales prove that every household eats more fish.|Turnover measures every unpaid contribution.|Costs always fall when revenue rises.|Revenue measures only one part of the effect.
Look at neighbouring sites.|Which comparison tests displaced activity?|Track changes at the original and alternative landings under comparable conditions.|Measure one managed site only.|Ignore new congestion outside the boundary.|Count planned sites as operating.|A boundary can hide risks moved elsewhere.
Set a condition for appraisal.|Which action fits an unsupported habitat claim?|Seek qualified evidence and keep the claim provisional.|Approve it because the proposal uses blue finance language.|Replace the claim with an unrelated turnover figure.|Treat all environmental concerns as resolved.|A responsible reviewer asks for evidence before relying on the claim.
            """),
        m('Verify benefit commitments over time',
            'Write an accountable community-benefit record with measures, grievance route and revision triggers.',
            """
Specify a benefit commitment in terms an affected worker can understand. Describe who qualifies, what the delivery is, the time window, the owner and how a person can ask for review. A broad promise of community jobs leaves unanswered whether work is accessible, paid, sustained and suited to people bearing project costs. Use measures that address those questions. [S84]

Consider a fictional agreement for twenty weekly discounted storage slots. Available slots, booked slots and successful use are different observations. If twelve of twenty eligible workers book and nine complete a use, report both stages. Identify whether the benefit reached processors who needed it at the intended hours. Track cost, wait and missed opportunities. [S81]

Include a transparent decision and review route. The terms may need revision if small users consistently cannot book, or if upkeep grows beyond available funding. Record an owner able to act, the evidence required and the timeframe. Keep accounting for adverse effects separately from benefit delivery so gains for one group do not conceal losses or rights concerns elsewhere. [S84]

Plan a simple follow-up with a dated register, aggregate measures and a safe way to report problems. Write both a public summary and a more detailed responsible-team entry using fictional information. Show what is still outstanding and identify the authority needed for any change to terms. A monitoring plan is useful when it tells someone what action follows a result. [S84] Include a route for people who applied unsuccessfully to explain access difficulties. Track whether the response changes the capacity, eligibility or timing of the offer.
            """,
            'A benefit agreement promises 20 discounted opportunities. Twelve eligible workers book and nine make a successful use. The dashboard records all 20 as served.',
            'Write a separate offer, booking and receipt measure and a response if small processors are excluded.',
            'Report the 20 offers, 12 bookings and nine uses with their denominators. Follow up on barriers through an accessible review route.',
            """
Specify a measurable commitment.|What does an actionable benefit promise include?|Recipient rules, delivery, timeframe, responsible role and review.|A general hope that everyone gains.|An untracked pledge number.|A project name without terms.|Clear commitments make follow-up possible.
Keep stages visible.|Which count represents completed use in the supplied case?|Nine successful uses.|Twenty available slots.|Twelve bookings.|Twenty registered online page views.|Availability and booking are earlier stages.
Identify reasons for low uptake.|What should the reviewer check when eligible small processors rarely book?|Hours, fees, booking requirements and usable capacity.|Only the colour of the advert.|Whether staff enjoyed training.|Whether the grant name is memorable.|Patterns of uptake need practical explanation.
Assign an action trigger.|Which trigger enables a decision?|Review if the next comparable period again shows few completed uses for small processors.|Decide whenever convenient.|Count every booking as permanently successful.|Stop measuring once plans are printed.|A trigger connects a defined result to follow-up.
Report adverse effects independently.|Why should access losses continue to be tracked when benefits reach some users?|Improvements for some can coexist with unresolved costs for others.|A positive total removes all costs.|Each benefit automatically compensates everyone.|Complaints count as completed benefits.|Distribution and accountability require seeing both gains and losses.
            """),
    ])
